In REDACTED.js, SSL configuration is managed through the ssl option in the connection configuration object [1][2]. Connection String SSL Options While you can provide a connection string (URL), the REDACTED.js driver processes SSL primarily through the options object [1][2]. Some common libpq-style parameters can be included in the connection string query parameters [3][4], such as sslmode, though full control over SSL behavior—especially certificate management—is typically handled by passing an object to the ssl option [1][5]. SSL Option Configuration The ssl option can be configured in several ways: 1. Boolean or String: You can set ssl to true (or a string like 'require' or 'prefer') for basic enablement [6][7][2]. 2. Object (Advanced Configuration): For more granular control, pass an object that matches the TLS options available in your environment (e.g., Node.js tls.connect options) [5][8]. - Common configuration: To disable certificate verification (often used in development or where verifiable certificates are unavailable), you can use: { ssl: { rejectUnauthorized: false } } [1][9][10]. - Custom CA: To provide a custom CA certificate, you can pass a ca option within the object (e.g., { ssl: { ca: [fs.readFileSync('path/to/cert.pem')] } }) [5][8]. Default Behavior The default value for the ssl option is false [6][7]. If not explicitly configured, the driver will not use SSL by default. Note on sslrootcert=system Postgres.js supports the sslrootcert=system option in the connection string [3], which is a shorthand introduced in PostgreSQL 16 to utilize the system's root certificates for verification [3]. When using connection strings with specific SSL requirements (like sslmode=verify-full), ensure your configuration correctly points to the necessary certificates, as the driver will look for them based on the provided ssl object or environmental context [3][4].

