# Establish a trusted Node client-IP boundary

## Affected sites
- `services/account/src/node.ts:78` — prioritizes the caller-supplied `CF-Connecting-IP` value and falls back to another forwarded header.
- `Caddyfile:51` — uses the default reverse-proxy header behavior, which sanitizes `X-Forwarded-*` but passes `CF-Connecting-IP` through.
- `authfn@0.2.0/dist/core/rate-limit.js:27` — uses the resolver result directly in each IP rate-limit key.

## Required outcome
- Every supported Node topology derives a stable client address from a trusted socket or proxy boundary.
- Untrusted requests cannot select a fresh rate-limit bucket by changing `CF-Connecting-IP` or `X-Forwarded-For`.

## Suggested approach
- Have the trusted proxy overwrite or remove incoming client-IP headers and consume only that sanitized value, or gate forwarded-header trust behind an explicit proxy configuration.
- Caddy header behavior: https://caddyserver.com/docs/caddyfile/directives/reverse_proxy#headers
````

